answers.body:
The correct way to do what you describe is #pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0 ; i < x.size(); i++) {
        x[i] += 1;
} This automatically splits the loop and gives each thread its own value of i. You have to be careful though as you can still easily introduce a race condition. #pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 1 ; i < x.size(); i++) {
        x[i] += x[i-1]; //race
} Suppose ThreadA is working on the iteration where i = 2 and ThreadB is working on the iteration where i = 3 . ThreadA and ThreadB both access x[2] at the same time, which as you noted is bad. It is up to you the programmer to ensure your OpenMP code is race free. There is not any automatic way of keeping your code safe.

