I don't know anything about openmp , but you are most assuredly blowing up your stack. Default stack space will vary from system to system, but with N == 1000 , you are trying to put three 2D arrays totaling 3 million double s on the stack. Assuming a double is 8 bytes, that's 24 million bytes, or just shy of 22.9MB.  There can't be many systems allowing that kind of stack space.  Instead, I'd recommend trying to grab that amount of memory from the heap.  Something like this: //double a[N][N],b[N][N],c[N][N];
double **a, **b, **c;
a = malloc(sizeof(double*) * N);
b = malloc(sizeof(double*) * N);
c = malloc(sizeof(double*) * N);

for (i=0; i<N; i++)
{
  a[i] = malloc(sizeof(double) * N);
  b[i] = malloc(sizeof(double) * N);
  c[i] = malloc(sizeof(double) * N);
}

// do your calculations

for (i=0; i<N; i++)
{
  free(a[i]);
  free(b[i]);
  free(c[i]);
}
free(a);
free(b);
free(c); I've verified on my machine at least, that with N == 1000 I crash right out of the gate with EXC_BAD_ACCESS when trying to place those arrays on the stack.  When I dynamically allocate the memory instead as shown above, I get no seg faults.

